WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.761
Module 3.

00:02.111 --> 00:04.665
Ethics, court rules and regulation.

00:05.015 --> 00:10.665
This module summarises the professional conduct framework for AI use in U.S.

00:10.665 --> 00:16.316
legal practice: ABA Formal Opinion 512, state guidance, court standing orders, continuing

00:16.316 --> 00:21.966
education developments and, for firms with EU exposure, the AI literacy duty.

00:23.716 --> 00:28.135
By the end of this module you will be able to:

00:28.135 --> 00:32.554
apply ABA Formal Opinion 512 to everyday AI use; know where

00:32.554 --> 00:36.974
to find state and court-specific requirements; understand informed consent and billing

00:36.974 --> 00:40.991
rules for AI; recognise when AI use must be disclosed.

00:42.741 --> 00:49.614
The central reference point is ABA Formal Opinion 512, issued on July 29, 2024.

00:49.964 --> 00:53.459
Its core message is that there are no special rules for AI.

00:53.809 --> 00:57.745
The existing Model Rules of Professional Conduct already apply.

00:58.095 --> 01:03.878
Competence, confidentiality, communication with clients, reasonable fees, candor toward the tribunal

01:03.878 --> 01:09.136
and supervision all apply when a lawyer uses generative AI.

01:09.486 --> 01:11.332
Let's take them one at a time.

01:13.082 --> 01:18.440
Under Rule 1.1, competence, you must have a reasonable understanding of the capabilities and

01:18.440 --> 01:23.415
limitations of the specific tool you use, and you must review its output.

01:23.765 --> 01:29.483
Under the confidentiality rules, 1.6, 1.9 c and 1.18 b, you must

01:29.483 --> 01:35.200
assess the risk of disclosure before putting client information into a tool.

01:35.550 --> 01:40.200
Under Rule 1.4, communication, you may need to tell clients about your

01:40.200 --> 01:44.850
AI use, particularly where it affects their matter or when they ask.

01:45.200 --> 01:49.130
Under Rule 1.5, fees, you bill for actual time, and you generally

01:49.130 --> 01:53.060
cannot charge a client for the time you spend learning a tool.

01:53.410 --> 01:58.472
Under the candor rules, 3.1, 3.3 and 8.4 c, you must not

01:58.472 --> 02:03.534
submit unverified AI output to a tribunal, and you must correct errors.

02:03.884 --> 02:09.346
And under Rules 5.1 and 5.3, managers must put AI policies in place, make

02:09.346 --> 02:14.809
sure lawyers and non-lawyers are trained, and vet the vendors the firm relies on.

02:16.559 --> 02:20.344
Two points in Opinion 512 tend to surprise lawyers.

02:20.694 --> 02:22.702
First, informed consent.

02:23.052 --> 02:27.898
Before a client's confidential information goes into a self-learning tool, one that may

02:27.898 --> 02:32.743
use the input to improve itself, the client must give informed consent, after

02:32.743 --> 02:37.216
you explain the risk that the information could surface in later outputs.

02:37.566 --> 02:41.816
The opinion says boilerplate language in an engagement letter is not enough.

02:42.165 --> 02:43.268
Second, billing.

02:43.618 --> 02:46.277
Hourly bills must reflect the actual time spent,

02:46.277 --> 02:48.936
not the time the task used to take.

02:49.286 --> 02:51.880
A flat fee may become unreasonable if AI

02:51.880 --> 02:54.150
lets you complete the work dramatically faster.

02:54.500 --> 02:58.892
And time spent learning a general AI tool is overhead, not billable

02:58.892 --> 03:02.918
time, unless the client specifically asked you to use that tool.

03:04.668 --> 03:07.686
Beyond the ABA, requirements vary by forum.

03:08.036 --> 03:11.670
Many state bars, including Florida and Texas, have issued their

03:11.670 --> 03:15.304
own AI ethics opinions, and they are not identical, so

03:15.304 --> 03:18.938
check the current text for every state where you practise.

03:19.288 --> 03:22.939
Some judges have standing orders requiring lawyers to disclose or

03:22.939 --> 03:26.590
certify their use of AI in filings, so check local

03:26.590 --> 03:29.876
rules and each judge's individual orders before you file.

03:30.226 --> 03:32.548
Continuing education is catching up too.

03:32.898 --> 03:38.640
New Jersey will require one technology credit every two years from January

03:38.640 --> 03:43.904
1, 2027, and its proposed definition of technology topics includes AI.

03:44.254 --> 03:49.202
And if your firm operates in the European Union, Article 4 of the

03:49.202 --> 03:54.150
EU AI Act has required organisations that deploy AI to ensure a sufficient

03:54.150 --> 03:58.337
level of AI literacy among their staff since February 2, 2025.

04:00.087 --> 04:05.161
Because the rules keep changing, every firm needs a simple regulatory watch routine.

04:05.511 --> 04:08.843
First, map your forums: list every state, court

04:08.843 --> 04:12.175
and agency where the firm files or advises.

04:12.525 --> 04:16.194
Second, assign an owner, one person responsible for

04:16.194 --> 04:19.862
tracking new opinions, standing orders and rule changes.

04:20.212 --> 04:24.572
Third, build a check into every filing: before anything is filed,

04:24.572 --> 04:28.931
confirm whether that judge or court requires AI disclosure or certification.

04:29.281 --> 04:35.144
And fourth, update quarterly, refreshing the firm's AI policy and training with anything new.

04:35.494 --> 04:39.030
This programme's quarterly update supports that routine, but

04:39.030 --> 04:42.124
responsibility for compliance stays with the firm.

04:43.874 --> 04:45.487
Let's recap the key points.

04:45.837 --> 04:50.389
Opinion 512: existing rules apply fully to AI use.

04:50.739 --> 04:55.452
Informed consent is needed before confidential data goes into self-learning tools.

04:55.802 --> 04:58.821
Bill actual time; learning a tool is overhead.

04:59.171 --> 05:02.770
Check state opinions and judges' standing orders for every forum.

05:04.520 --> 05:05.681
Knowledge check.

05:06.031 --> 05:08.960
A client's engagement letter contains a general line

05:08.960 --> 05:11.522
saying the firm 'may use technology tools'.

05:11.872 --> 05:16.818
Is that informed consent to put their confidential data into a self-learning AI tool?

05:17.168 --> 05:19.966
Option A: Yes, the client signed it.

05:20.316 --> 05:26.342
Option B: No, Opinion 512 says boilerplate is not enough for self-learning tools.

05:26.692 --> 05:30.256
Option C: Yes, if the tool is from a well-known vendor.

05:30.606 --> 05:33.381
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

05:39.131 --> 05:40.663
The answer is option B.

05:41.013 --> 05:44.508
Informed consent requires explaining the specific risk.

05:44.858 --> 05:48.480
Generic engagement-letter language does not meet that standard.
